#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433175 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119944 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e28b26-0eb7-51ed-bcf9-2660b98a6028" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Line to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01f9c607-a00d-5f26-9c10-092b5fb19c5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,0,1⟩+u⟨-2,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433156} {:answer/id #uuid "35a27e71-b8e5-549e-97ab-d093597bcb5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,2⟩+u⟨1,-1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433157} {:answer/id #uuid "3e141537-57e7-5d24-b24a-a74eaabb1717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-2,1⟩+u⟨4,-1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433158} {:answer/id #uuid "7c3eb033-52d1-5b96-94b9-64ac07d6af16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,0,1⟩+u⟨1,-1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433164} {:answer/id #uuid "a8a9f5e6-8ed5-5aa2-8980-b3e5d5b6e091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,2⟩+u⟨4,-1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c3eb033-52d1-5b96-94b9-64ac07d6af16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,0,1⟩+u⟨1,-1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433164} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb45a89e-3bde-5251-b24c-b1bd686d3497" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8d1a00d-2251-5725-a02b-3b2319318fd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-126832" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨t+3,\\,1-t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩$ at the point $P(4,0,1),$ valid for all real values of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $P(4,0,1)$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point,  the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nR(u)=r({t}_{0})+u\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨4,0,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨t+3,1-t,{t}^{2}⟩ & =⟨4,0,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the components yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}t+3=4 \\\\ 1-t=0 \\\\ {t}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nFrom the first equation, we get $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other equation. Therefore,  $P(4,0,1)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(1).$\n\nWe calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+3),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1) & =⟨1,-1,2(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point  $P(4,0,1)$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=⟨4,0,1⟩+u⟨1,-1,2⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229139 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68148c93-9139-5deb-8a53-a05d63ec5930" :knowledge-point/title "Using Linearity Properties of Linear Differential Operators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55b4ddff-c263-5dbe-b540-8610072861cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263737} {:answer/id #uuid "5ee01579-2851-5ce8-b8b9-ecb5b093cc56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263823} {:answer/id #uuid "b2212b32-79f2-572f-bd4e-74fce4becbfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264531} {:answer/id #uuid "e5c45080-32dd-5fd6-9485-fb31091cb2f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265002} {:answer/id #uuid "eea0ecf4-e21b-5ca5-9dc1-d0c68c00439c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ee01579-2851-5ce8-b8b9-ecb5b093cc56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263823} :answer-field/id #uuid "e471ca41-6d52-5222-9793-59466bf7b5b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bfd36b47-f530-50b4-b4b7-119c298890d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-130080" :question/problem "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})=2-{sin}^{2}⁡x$ and $L({y}_{2})=3{cos}^{2}⁡x$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L(3{y}_{1}-{y}_{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(3{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & =3L({y}_{1})-L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (2-{sin}^{2}⁡x)-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =6-3{sin}^{2}⁡x-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =6-3({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =6-3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054833 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1e93acb-6c0b-5263-9df9-9dcc00680845" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unions and Intersections of (Countably) Infinite Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f9404ea-93a6-52ed-a4d6-367243aa4e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433160} {:answer/id #uuid "62c57b84-928c-5133-b786-712eefb8d26e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433161} {:answer/id #uuid "8a4ed120-03c1-59d6-a981-4def59b8adad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433166} {:answer/id #uuid "8d70a1cd-4456-5ae3-b1c0-6ff128292655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433167} {:answer/id #uuid "d3f58a65-9c0a-5a6d-943e-ddc8a004490c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{4}{3}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a4ed120-03c1-59d6-a981-4def59b8adad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433166} :answer-field/id #uuid "72992156-232d-5e59-a853-f830c746fc70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0553cd0-23ab-5839-b32e-fb9520332a9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-181534" :question/problem "Let $I=N$ and ${S}_{i}=(0,\\frac{2i}{2i-1}),$ then ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{S}_{i}=$" :question/worked-solution "The intersection of a family of indexed sets $\\{{A}_{i}\\}_{i\\in I}$ contains all elements that belong to *all* sets in our family:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\in U\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{i}\\,\\,\\text{for all }{A}_{i},\\,\\,i\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is a universal set.\n\nLet's now write down a few instances of our indexed sets:\n\n- if $i=1,$ we have ${S}_{1}=(0,2)$\n\n- if $i=2,$ we have ${S}_{2}=(0,\\frac{4}{3})$\n\n- if $i=3,$ we have ${S}_{3}=(0,\\frac{6}{5})$\n\n- if $i=4,$ we have ${S}_{4}=(0,\\frac{8}{7})$\n\nand so on.\n\nNotice that as $i$ increases, \n\n- the left endpoint of the intervals is $0$ for all the sets,\n\n- the right endpoint of the intervals is greater than $1$ and approaches $1$ from the right, never reaching it.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⋂}_{i\\in I}{S}_{i}=(0,1].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305140 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229167 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef7a2bbc-4172-591e-98eb-f7586d3ae06b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Sinusoidal Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "577ddd51-e3bd-5790-981c-b2f4d35144fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302989}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "577ddd51-e3bd-5790-981c-b2f4d35144fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302989} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2d672de-2a9b-5e87-8553-d2afe5b67ad3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304651} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6eb7988a-9925-541c-a1be-439e5577b8e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{19}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6eb7988a-9925-541c-a1be-439e5577b8e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{19}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303132} :answer-field/id #uuid "f01e1a9b-a805-5e89-ae02-8bb83060bea3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "51ccf9c3-0db9-5b22-9dee-31cd44f9c3bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-332894" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+12y=-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=A{e}^{-3x}\\,+\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n4\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+12{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n4(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))+12(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))=-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(12\\beta +12\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(-12\\alpha +12\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}12\\alpha +12\\beta =-8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ -12\\alpha +12\\beta =11\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{19}{24},\\beta =\\frac{1}{8}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{19}{24}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+(-\\frac{19}{24})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(\\frac{1}{8})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433173 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "008275fa-715e-57ea-bc82-5a6102db2a5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433155} {:answer/id #uuid "59f9131e-98fe-5931-bf40-b384cfc12aca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433159} {:answer/id #uuid "75750201-9871-5333-9d36-628baad463f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433162} {:answer/id #uuid "75b5793f-7a42-5e9f-b867-97cb0a94ee05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433163} {:answer/id #uuid "856794aa-f420-58cc-9b35-d295a27c06b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75b5793f-7a42-5e9f-b867-97cb0a94ee05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433163} :answer-field/id #uuid "375de494-0474-59f2-a5cb-d4f2d3d924b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46f6694f-8103-554b-a6d3-9a98e8c2973d" :question/math-academy-id "q-34711" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nII. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$  has an infinite number of nonzero solutions.\n\nIII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+5{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{2}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statement III is false, while statements I and II are true."}]}
